  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAs another subtle shot of CAA brings light lake effect rain showers \n(with some lightning at times no less!) to the eastern U.P. early \nthis morning, with satellite imagery showing mostly clear skies \nacross most of the area as temperatures have dropped down into the \n40s to even upper 30s at spots. While the lake effect cloud \ncover/showers over the east today will keep things warmer/more \ninsulated before dawn, thinking we could see some of the cold spots \nin the interior west drop down to around 30 before sunrise this \nmorning. With high pressure ridging moving overhead later today, \nexpect the lake effect clouds/precip to wrap up across the eastern \nU.P later this morning, with no more than a trace of rainfall \nexpected from it. With antecedent cooler air overhead, expect the \nhighs today to only get into the 50s across Upper MI. Likewise, \nexpect the winds to dwindle today via the incoming high pressure \nridging moving overhead late in the day. \n\nThat being said, more active weather is expected to return tonight \nas a Clipper low phasing with another northern Canadian low digs \ntowards the Upper Great Lakes late tonight through Tuesday. While \nmost medium range QPF guidance keeps the area dry tonight, with the \nlatest NAM and several CAMs now showing mainly rainfall moving in \nfrom west to east along a subtle shortwave passing by tonight into \nTuesday, thinking we could see some light rain showers (that \npotentially even mix with or over to snow at times!) across the area \ntonight into Tuesday morning. However, capping the forecasted rain \nchances at 50% for tonight as antecedent dry air from the high \npressure ridging just leaving the area will need to be overcome. \nOverall, not thinking the area will receive much rainfall, as even \nthe latest REFS shows a 0% chance for over a tenth of an inch of \nrain. That being said, it may be enough to at least make the ground \na little wet. As the phased low approaches from northern Ontario on \nTuesday, we may see rain chances increase for the eastern U.P. \nthroughout the day as synoptic support strengthens. In addition to \nthe rain chances, expect winds to pick up from the south on Tuesday, \nparticularly over the eastern half of the U.P. where south to \nsouthwest wind gusts of 25 to occasionally even 35 mph are possible \nby the afternoon hours. As the cold sector of the phased low moves \nin Tuesday night and reinforcing CAA continues even into Thursday \nmorning, expect to see some light lake effect rain showers (with \npotentially some rumbles of thunder at times) over the eastern U.P. \nagain; expect nothing more than a couple hundreths of an inch of \nprecipitation with the lake effect rainfall though as delta-Ts will \nbe in the lower teens. Yet, even despite the CAA rolling into the \narea, expect above normal temperatures to return to the U.P. for the \nmid-week as highs reach into the 60s to around 70 in the south \ncentral; it looks like it won't be until Thursday when 850mb temps \nare coolest when high temperatures return to normal (highs in the \nmid 50s to mid 60s). \n\nWhile high pressure ridging moving in late this week is looking to \nbring dry weather back over us for at least Friday, an impressively \ndeep Clipper (~995mb) lifts from the Rockies late this week and \neither splits into two separate lows or continues along the southern \nCanadian Prairies through the weekend. Regardless, this Clipper low \nis looking to be responsible for well above normal temperatures \nreturning to the area this upcoming weekend, with highs into the 70s \npossible by next Sunday.",
